A||||19||loafer87gt||Is the rear wing functional?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||05-20-2000||05:48 PM||||Well after my little mishap I have been trying ot decide whether or not to put the rear wing back on. From a looks standpoint, I kind of think the car looks a bit cleaner without the wing, but I was just wondering if the wing serves any purpose for aerodynamics or downforce? DOes anyone know what the drag coefficient is with and without the wing, thanks guys and gals, later||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-20-2000||05:58 PM||||Haven't seen one in awhile. I believe they are mostly decorative in the factory configuration.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sillhouette.com||05-20-2000||07:34 PM||||Nope that rear wing is extra weight and I don't even think that it looks that good.  The way I know it that I put a fiero into a wind tunnel. The downforce from the wing is cancelled out from the upforce that is created through the engine bay.  Also by the time the air would have enough force to push the rear down it already has passed the car.  Also make sure you don't take your fiero much faster than 171 mph.  I found that the car lifts off at that speed.<P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||05-20-2000||07:51 PM||||How about some data?  Drag coefficient or anything else we might understand?||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||05-20-2000||08:33 PM||||That's funny Sillhouette, because mine doesn't launch until about 173.  I always try to hit at least 160 down main street on the way to work.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||phillip||05-20-2000||10:11 PM||||if your library has back issues of road and track go to the late '84 issues for the road test of the gt when it first came out. i remember them mentioning some cd numbers and how the wing affected it but i don't remember what they were. or go a year earlier to when the '84 came out and compare cd numbers bettween the two articles. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||gixxer||05-20-2000||10:42 PM||||The airflow is completely disturbed and turbulent behind the rear window. Since a wing needs smooth airflow to work in, I'd judge the Fiero wing to be practically useless(as are almost all low mounted wings on passenger cars). To add insult to injury, the Fiero wing has several large indented stiffeners on the lower surface. If by chance, some usable air were to pass over(and under) the wing, it would be disturbed by the indentations and the wing would stall, thereby developing little or no downforce. So the only downforce that is  possibly available is if some air is deflected up off the TOP of the wing. Rather like a spoiler. <BR>Oh yeah, the original 2M4s' CD was .377. The '88 GT CD was .38.<BR>For more info on wings, please see: <A HREF="http://www.lerc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/short.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lerc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/short.html</A> <BR>Look under "LIFT"<BR>They take special pains to debunk the "fact" that the Bernoulli effect is what makes airplanes fly!<BR>(Downwash Rules!)<BR>Cheers!     ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ed||05-20-2000||10:48 PM||||I'll answer my own question here because of Phillip, although I'd still like to know what Shillhouette.com found.<P>Brooklands put out a book called "Pontiac Fiero 1984-1988" in their Road Test series.  It is a compilation of articles.  Some articles say that coupes had about a 0.41 cd without and about 0.35 with the wing.  Witzenburg's book has the GT/fastback at 0.36.  Nothing about a GT cd with wing that I could find.  <P>What did you find, Sillhouette? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Sillhouette.com||05-20-2000||10:56 PM||||Man that site from NASA brings back first year aerodynamics memories from college.  I will get the CD values sometimes on tuesday.  It's at RSMs shop that will not be open til tuesday because of a long weekend here in Canada.  The car that was tested was an 87 GT with  brand new paint on it and no wing.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||05-20-2000||10:58 PM||||In laymans terms, I've found that it flaps in the wind at 100 MPH. Almost looks silly.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||05-20-2000||11:16 PM||||Quote from Road and Track magazine about drag coefficient "The original fiero with the 13 inch wheels and tires has a Cx of 0.377; add 14-inch wheels and tires and that figure climbs to 0.406. With the pace car nose and 14-inch wheels and tires the coefficient of drag drops to 0.372 and falls still further to 0.350 with the addition of the rocker panel extensions and rear wing". Don't you love it when someone gives you the facts? The wing does help a little with reducing the drag coefficient.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||05-20-2000||11:19 PM||||I've noticed that a waxed wing with water beads on it, has no air on the top side of the wing until about 80-90 mph. Then the water beads start to slowly move aft. I suspect that the wing provides looks rather than actual down force. Maybe if you raise the wing and get to 150mph it would help.||||reg||12
Z||000012||2birds||05-21-2000||12:03 AM||||Of course it serves a functional purpose.  I find that at night time, with the mirror on the night setting, I can't see the car behind me if it's within 1/4 mile, unless I flip the mirror back to day to catch the headlight glare.  So the wing saves me the HUGE effort of having to change mirror positions as it gets dark.  <P>Seriously though, IMHO, I think a fastback looks kind of naked without it, while a notchback looks cleaner without it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phaeton||05-21-2000||12:18 AM||||The normal airflow over most vehicles results in two large whirlwinds coming sidways off the rear corners of the car.<P>Motion takes energy, the less motion the less energy used and the lower the CD.  Downforce adds to the CD.<BR>The wing evens out the airflow off the hood to randomize the vortices on the corners into a ball of high pressure directly behind the Fiero.  Not a big difference though, keeping air out from under the Fiero would probably do more good to the CD than the wing.||||reg||1
Z||000014||wkayl||05-21-2000||02:14 AM||||Yes the wing is functional. <BR>It blocks the headlights of the stupid people who pull up to your tail at stoplights at night.  Tall Fiero drivers just have to sink down into the seat and short Fiero drivers have to do the goose neck.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Haze_Performance||05-21-2000||04:25 AM||||Well if anyone would like to sell there UGLY or USELESS stock wing please email me. Haze_Mustang@AutomotiveAuthority.com Thank You||||reg||14
Z||000016||phillip||05-21-2000||05:45 AM||||haze, if you're into mustangs and have access to one, try setting a saleen mustang wing ( late '80s- early '90s style) for a coupe/conv. on the car to see how it looks. thats what i have. easier to find and the trunk demensions are so close that it went on with no trunk leaks (mounts out side of seal) and looks pretty good. different any way and easier to find. got mine $150 new. since i just got the indy it gives them different looks. if i get another with no wing i plan to put a mustang f-40 wing on it. back when fieros were being made some one had one that i believe had a f-40 on it. kind of looked like a mini superbird.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||05-21-2000||06:14 AM||||Motor Trend, February 1985, stated that <BR>the wing was needed on the Fiero GT to <BR>reach lower drag numbers.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||05-21-2000||01:19 PM||||The wings on most cars (exceptions include F1, NASCAR, etc) are cosmetic only.  Either a car is built with enough downforce to do the job or it isn't.  The C5 Vette is a perfect example.  It'll stick to the road like glue yet has no wing (and it's not an option, either).  I think most Fieros look naked without a wing.  I sure like mine, but I don't think it does anything.  (I'm a long winded bastard, aren't I? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000019||Old Lar||05-22-2000||07:39 PM||||The wing are for looks on a Fiero.  The slight changes in drag coefficients would be imperceptable at normal driving speeds.  I really doubt that the wing adjustments on any of NASCAR race cars would make any differences in handeling at 70-80 mph.  However at 150-200 mph it makes a big difference.<P>I agree with 2birds that the GT looks naked without it, the coupe looks good both ways.  Personal preference dictates if you have a wing or not.||||reg||11
